<!-- Key Design Decisions -->
<section id="key-design-decisions" class="cs-section">
  <div class="section-eyebrow"><span>Key Design Decisions</span></div>
  <h2 class="section-heading">The Moments That Mattered</h2>
  <div class="section-row">
    <div class="section-col">
      <ul class="cs-bullet-list">
        <li>
          <div>
            <strong>Slowing down to move faster</strong>
            <span>The team culture pushed hard for immediate delivery. One of the most important decisions was advocating to pause and properly understand the problem first — running research, doing the heuristic review, and mapping the full journey before a single screen was redesigned. It wasn't always a comfortable conversation, but it led to a fundamentally better outcome.</span>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <div>
            <strong>Pushing back with evidence, not opinion</strong>
            <span>Stakeholders weren't used to being challenged. Rather than presenting opinions, the approach was to let the research and heuristic findings do the talking — framing pushback around user impact and business risk rather than design preference. That shift changed the dynamic of stakeholder conversations.</span>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <div>
            <strong>Designing for now and for later</strong>
            <span>Smart Charging was the immediate brief, but designing it in isolation would have created more fragmentation down the line. A key decision was to collaborate closely with a Service Designer to align the near-term work with a longer-term energy management vision — so the V1G work wouldn't need to be undone when V2G arrived.</span>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <div>
            <strong>Cross-design alignment as a foundation</strong>
            <span>With multiple teams owning different parts of the experience, aligning with other designers on a shared vision was non-negotiable. Without that, each team would continue building in their own direction.</span>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
